---
import DocLayout from '../../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
---

<DocLayout title="Layout" description="Layout components in the Farn design system — cards, section transitions, and structural patterns">
  <h1>Layout</h1>
  <p>
    Structural components for organising content on a page. Cards surface discrete pieces of
    content in a scannable grid; section transitions create intentional visual breaks between
    full-width areas.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Cards ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->

  <h2 id="cards">Cards <span class="badge badge-published">stable</span></h2>
  <p>
    Entry points that surface content and invite action. Background, border, and border-radius
    are driven by <code>--card-*</code> Tier-3 tokens — retheme in one place without touching
    the semantic layer.
  </p>

  <div class="card-demo-row">
    <div class="card card-demo-item">
      <p class="demo-label">Default</p>
      <p>Background fill, no visible border. Use for most card contexts.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-outlined card-demo-item">
      <p class="demo-label">Outlined</p>
      <p>Adds a <code>--color-border</code> edge for same-level surfaces.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card card-highlight card-demo-item">
      <p class="demo-label">Highlight</p>
      <p>High-contrast background — adapts per theme.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Anatomy</h3>
  <p>
    When anatomy children are present the wrapper's padding is removed automatically via
    <code>:has()</code> — each section carries its own <code>var(--card-padding)</code>.
    <strong><code>.card-media</code> must be the first child</strong> so its top corners
    align with the card's border-radius.
  </p>

  <div class="card-demo-section">
    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-media">
        <div class="card-media-placeholder" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card-header">
        <p class="demo-card-heading">Fern Valley Trail</p>
        <p class="demo-card-meta">3.2 km · Moderate</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <p class="demo-card-text">A shaded loop through old-growth forest with seasonal wildflower meadows along the ridge.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card-footer">
        <button class="btn btn-p btn-sm">View trail</button>
        <button class="btn btn-g btn-sm">Save</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="accordion">
    <details>
      <summary>Reference — Cards</summary>
      <div class="accordion-panel">

        <h3>Variants</h3>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Variant</th><th>Class</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Default</td><td><code>.card</code></td><td>Article lists, feature grids, project indexes. Background: <code>--card-bg</code> → <code>--color-bg-panel</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Outlined</td><td><code>.card-outlined</code></td><td>Adds <code>var(--color-border)</code> edge when the surrounding surface matches the card's background.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Highlight</td><td><code>.card-highlight</code></td><td>Featured or promoted content. Background adapts per theme: Forest (<code>--fo2-forest</code>) in light, Sage (<code>--fo0-sage</code>) in dark — text inverts to maintain contrast in both modes.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Interactive</td><td><code>.card-interactive</code></td><td>Any card that is a navigation target or action trigger — adds hover, focus, and active states.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>Anatomy</h3>
        <pre><code>&lt;div class="card"&gt;
  &lt;div class="card-media"&gt;&lt;img src="trail.jpg" alt="…"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="card-header"&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Title&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="card-body"&gt;&lt;p&gt;Description&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="card-footer"&gt;
    &lt;button class="btn btn-p btn-sm"&gt;Action&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>

        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>.card-media</code></td><td>Top image / visual area</td><td>Must be first child. <code>overflow:hidden</code> clips to card radius.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.card-header</code></td><td>Title and metadata row</td><td>Use semantic heading elements (<code>h2</code>–<code>h4</code>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.card-body</code></td><td>Main content area</td><td><code>flex: 1</code> — expands to fill in equal-height grids.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.card-footer</code></td><td>Actions row</td><td><code>margin-top: auto</code> pins to bottom. Divider appears when preceded by <code>.card-body</code>.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>Interactive cards</h3>
        <p>
          Add <code>.card-interactive</code> to any card that is a navigation target or action trigger.
          Works on both <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> (with <code>tabindex="0"</code>) and <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>.
        </p>
        <pre><code>&lt;!-- div card --&gt;
&lt;div class="card card-interactive" tabindex="0"&gt; … &lt;/div&gt;

&lt;!-- anchor card (whole card is the link) --&gt;
&lt;a href="/trail/fern-valley" class="card card-interactive"&gt; … &lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>

        <h3>Card grid</h3>
        <p>
          <code>.card-grid</code> creates a responsive auto-fit grid. Minimum column width: 280px.
          Cards collapse to a single column on narrow viewports.
        </p>
        <pre><code>&lt;div class="card-grid"&gt;
  &lt;div class="card card-interactive"&gt; … &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="card card-interactive"&gt; … &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="card card-interactive"&gt; … &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>

        <h3>Token reference</h3>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Default</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>--card-bg</code></td><td><code>var(--color-bg-panel)</code></td><td>Default card background</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--card-hover-bg</code></td><td><code>var(--color-bg-inset)</code></td><td>Background on hover (interactive cards)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--card-border</code></td><td><code>transparent</code></td><td>Border colour — <code>.card-outlined</code> overrides to <code>--color-border</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--card-radius</code></td><td><code>var(--radius-lg)</code></td><td>Border radius for card and media</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--card-padding</code></td><td><code>var(--space-md)</code></td><td>Padding for bare cards and all anatomy sections</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--card-highlight-bg</code></td><td><code>--fo2-forest</code> (light) · <code>--fo0-sage</code> (dark)</td><td>Background for <code>.card-highlight</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--card-highlight-border</code></td><td><code>transparent</code></td><td>Border for <code>.card-highlight</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--card-highlight-text</code></td><td><code>--bm2-birch</code> (light) · <code>--in0-void</code> (dark)</td><td>Primary text on highlight card</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--card-highlight-text-secondary</code></td><td><code>--bm1-mist</code> (light) · <code>--in1-iron</code> (dark)</td><td>Secondary text on highlight card</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--card-highlight-text-tertiary</code></td><td><code>--bm0-sand</code> (light) · <code>--in2-slate</code> (dark)</td><td>Tertiary text on highlight card</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>Overriding tokens</h3>
        <pre><code>/* Tighter padding for a dense list layout */
.article-list &#123;
  --card-padding: var(--space-sm);
&#125;

/* Pill cards in a marketing section */
.feature-grid &#123;
  --card-radius: var(--radius-xl);
&#125;</code></pre>

        <h3>CSS reference</h3>
        <p>Shipped in <code>dist/farn-components.css</code>. Load alongside <code>farn.css</code> or <code>farn-tokens.css</code>.</p>
        <pre><code>.card-grid &#123;
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
  gap: var(--space-md);
&#125;

.card &#123;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  background: var(--card-bg);
  border: 1px solid var(--card-border);
  border-radius: var(--card-radius);
  padding: var(--card-padding);
&#125;
.card:has(&gt; .card-header, &gt; .card-body, &gt; .card-footer, &gt; .card-media) &#123;
  padding: 0;
&#125;

.card-outlined  &#123; border-color: var(--color-border); &#125;
.card-highlight &#123; background: var(--card-highlight-bg); border-color: var(--card-highlight-border); &#125;

.card-interactive &#123;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: background var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out);
&#125;
.card-interactive:hover         &#123; background: var(--card-hover-bg); &#125;
.card-interactive:focus-visible &#123; outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent); outline-offset: 3px; &#125;
.card-interactive:active        &#123; transform: scale(0.99); &#125;</code></pre>

      </div>
    </details>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Section Transitions ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->

  <h2 id="section-transitions">Section Transitions <span class="badge badge-beta">beta</span></h2>
  <p>
    Intentional visual breaks between full-width page sections — signalling a shift in content,
    depth, or energy. All patterns consume the semantic token layer so transitions stay coherent
    as the palette evolves. Use one primary pattern per page.
  </p>

  <div class="demo-wave" role="group" aria-label="Sine wave divider live demo">
    <div class="demo-wave-top" data-theme="dark">
      <p class="demo-label">Preceding section</p>
      <p class="demo-section-desc">Wave at the bottom — fill matches the section below.</p>
      <div class="section-wave" data-scroll-reveal aria-hidden="true">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 1200 80" preserveAspectRatio="none">
          <path d="M0,40 C300,80 900,0 1200,40 L1200,80 L0,80 Z"
                fill="var(--color-section-next)" opacity="0.4"/>
          <path d="M0,45 C300,85 900,5 1200,45 L1200,80 L0,80 Z"
                fill="var(--color-section-next)"/>
        </svg>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="demo-wave-bottom" data-theme="light">
      <p class="demo-label">Next section</p>
      <p class="demo-section-desc">Override <code>--color-section-next</code> to match this background.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="accordion">
    <details>
      <summary>Full reference — Section Transitions</summary>
      <div class="accordion-panel">

        <h3>Patterns</h3>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Classes</th><th>Character</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Layered overlap</td><td><code>.overlap-preceding</code> / <code>.overlap-section</code> / <code>.overlap-card</code></td><td>Dimensional, modern</td><td>Dark hero → light content; featured CTA panels</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sine wave</td><td><code>.section-wave</code></td><td>Organic, rhythmic</td><td>Repeated section breaks; any natural flowing transition</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Convex arc</td><td><code>.section-arc</code></td><td>Refined, quiet</td><td>Centred hero layouts; calm lens-like elegance</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Organic blob</td><td><code>.section-blob</code></td><td>Expressive, freeform</td><td>High-emphasis brand moments; one-off expressive breaks</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Diagonal cut</td><td><code>.section-skew</code></td><td>Sharp, directional</td><td>CTA sections; forward-momentum transitions</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Stacked card reveal</td><td><code>.stack-container</code> / <code>.stack-section</code></td><td>Cinematic, layered</td><td>Long scroll journeys with 3–5 landmark sections</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p><strong>Use one primary type per page.</strong> A second type is acceptable for a single high-emphasis moment (e.g., sine waves throughout with one layered overlap at a CTA). More than two distinct types creates visual noise.</p>

        <!-- Layered overlap -->
        <h3 id="layered-overlap">Layered overlap</h3>
        <p>
          The only pure-CSS pattern — no SVG. The next section or a card bleeds upward into the
          preceding section using a negative <code>margin-top</code> combined with
          <code>border-radius</code> on the top corners.
        </p>

        <div class="demo-overlap-a" role="group" aria-label="Section overlap live demo">
          <div class="demo-section-before" data-theme="dark">
            <p class="demo-label">Preceding section</p>
            <p class="demo-section-desc">Dark area — never add <code>overflow:hidden</code> here.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="demo-section-after overlap-section">
            <p class="demo-label">Overlapping section</p>
            <p class="demo-section-desc">Rounded top corners float over the section above.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <pre><code>&lt;section class="overlap-preceding"&gt;
  &lt;!-- Hero — never overflow:hidden --&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;

&lt;section class="overlap-section" style="background: var(--color-bg-panel);"&gt;
  &lt;!-- Slides up over the hero --&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;</code></pre>

        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Class / attribute</th><th>Element</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>.overlap-preceding</code></td><td>Any block</td><td>Sets <code>overflow: visible</code> explicitly. Prevents accidental <code>overflow:hidden</code> clipping the overlap.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.overlap-section</code></td><td><code>&lt;section&gt;</code></td><td>Full section that slides up. Sets <code>z-index</code>, negative margin, rounded top corners. Consumer sets <code>background</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.overlap-card</code></td><td><code>&lt;div&gt;</code></td><td>Card variant — floats upward with Tier-1 entry animation (opacity + translateY + shadow grow).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-scroll-reveal</code></td><td><code>.overlap-card</code></td><td>Opts into the shared IntersectionObserver. Adds <code>.is-visible</code> on first entry.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><strong>Critical:</strong> <code>overflow:hidden</code> on <code>.overlap-preceding</code> or any ancestor will clip the overlapping element entirely.</p>

        <h4>Layered overlap tokens</h4>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Default</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>--overlap-section-radius</code></td><td><code>var(--radius-xl)</code></td><td>Top corners on <code>.overlap-section</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--overlap-section-offset</code></td><td><code>60px</code></td><td>How far <code>.overlap-section</code> bleeds upward</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--overlap-card-radius</code></td><td><code>var(--card-radius)</code></td><td>Border radius on <code>.overlap-card</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--overlap-card-offset</code></td><td><code>80px</code></td><td>How far <code>.overlap-card</code> bleeds upward</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--overlap-card-duration</code></td><td><code>0.7s</code></td><td>Entry animation duration</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <!-- Sine wave -->
        <h3 id="sine-wave">Sine wave</h3>
        <p>
          An SVG divider using cubic bezier curves. Two layered paths create the illusion of a
          receding tide. The wave sits absolutely positioned at the bottom of its parent section
          and fills in the next section's background colour.
        </p>
        <pre><code>&lt;section style="position: relative; padding-bottom: var(--wave-height);
             --color-section-next: var(--color-bg-panel);"&gt;
  &lt;div class="section-wave" data-scroll-reveal aria-hidden="true"&gt;
    &lt;svg viewBox="0 0 1200 80" preserveAspectRatio="none"&gt;
      &lt;!-- depth layer first (SVG paints in document order) --&gt;
      &lt;path d="M0,40 C300,80 900,0 1200,40 L1200,80 L0,80 Z"
            fill="var(--color-section-next)" opacity="0.4"/&gt;
      &lt;path d="M0,45 C300,85 900,5 1200,45 L1200,80 L0,80 Z"
            fill="var(--color-section-next)"/&gt;
    &lt;/svg&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;</code></pre>

        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Default</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>--wave-height</code></td><td><code>80px</code></td><td>SVG rendered height and required parent <code>padding-bottom</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--color-section-next</code></td><td><code>var(--color-bg-panel)</code></td><td>Fill colour — set on the parent to match the destination background</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Change amplitude by adjusting control-point Y values. Equal and opposite offsets (<code>C300,80 900,0</code>) produce a symmetric wave. To reverse direction, place <code>.section-wave</code> at the top of the <em>next</em> section and apply <code>transform: scaleY(-1)</code> to it (not <code>rotateX</code>).</p>

        <!-- Convex arc -->
        <h3 id="convex-arc">Convex arc</h3>
        <p>
          A single quadratic bezier curve spanning the full width. Calmer than the sine wave —
          one smooth dome suited to centred hero layouts. The convex variant animates with
          <code>animation-timeline: view()</code> (static fallback for unsupported browsers and
          <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>). The concave variant is always static.
        </p>
        <pre><code>&lt;!-- Convex (dome) --&gt;
&lt;section style="position: relative; padding-bottom: var(--arc-height);
             --color-section-next: var(--color-bg-panel);"&gt;
  &lt;div class="section-arc" aria-hidden="true"&gt;
    &lt;svg viewBox="0 0 1200 80" preserveAspectRatio="none"&gt;
      &lt;path d="M0,80 Q600,0 1200,80 Z" fill="var(--color-section-next)"/&gt;
    &lt;/svg&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;

&lt;!-- Concave (bowl) — redrawn path, always static --&gt;
&lt;div class="section-arc section-arc--concave" aria-hidden="true"&gt;
  &lt;svg viewBox="0 0 1200 80" preserveAspectRatio="none"&gt;
    &lt;path d="M0,0 Q600,80 1200,0 L1200,80 L0,80 Z" fill="var(--color-section-next)"/&gt;
  &lt;/svg&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>

        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Default</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>--arc-height</code></td><td><code>80px</code></td><td>SVG rendered height and required parent <code>padding-bottom</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--color-section-next</code></td><td><code>var(--color-bg-panel)</code></td><td>Fill colour — set on the parent to match the destination background</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <!-- Organic blob -->
        <h3 id="organic-blob">Organic blob</h3>
        <p>
          A freeform SVG boundary — no mathematical regularity. Two slightly offset paths
          layered in document order soften the edge. The blob fades in on scroll (opacity only;
          no translate or morph — irregular shapes look chaotic in motion). Use at most once per
          page for a high-emphasis brand moment.
        </p>
        <pre><code>&lt;section style="position: relative; padding-bottom: var(--blob-height);
             --color-section-next: var(--color-bg-panel);"&gt;
  &lt;div class="section-blob" data-scroll-reveal aria-hidden="true"&gt;
    &lt;svg viewBox="0 0 1200 100" preserveAspectRatio="none"&gt;
      &lt;!-- depth layer first (SVG paints in document order) --&gt;
      &lt;path d="M0,60 C150,25 200,80 400,55 C600,28 700,75 900,50 C1050,28 1150,65 1200,45 L1200,100 L0,100 Z"
            fill="var(--color-section-next)" opacity="0.45"/&gt;
      &lt;path d="M0,65 C150,30 200,85 400,60 C600,33 700,80 900,55 C1050,33 1150,70 1200,50 L1200,100 L0,100 Z"
            fill="var(--color-section-next)"/&gt;
    &lt;/svg&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;</code></pre>

        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Default</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>--blob-height</code></td><td><code>100px</code></td><td>SVG rendered height and required parent <code>padding-bottom</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--blob-duration</code></td><td><code>900ms</code></td><td>Fade-in duration — longer than other patterns to suit the organic character</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--color-section-next</code></td><td><code>var(--color-bg-panel)</code></td><td>Fill colour — set on the parent to match the destination background</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><strong>Draw paths in Figma or Inkscape — do not hand-author blob paths.</strong> Export the <code>d</code> attribute directly from your design tool. The silhouette can echo a motif elsewhere in the design (a landscape, terrain, or product shape). The example paths above are illustrative; replace them with exported paths for production use.</p>

        <!-- Diagonal cut -->
        <h3 id="diagonal-cut">Diagonal cut</h3>
        <p>
          A single angled line divides two sections — sharp, directional, and energetic. Three
          implementation approaches are available in increasing precision. Only approach 2 ships
          as a CSS class; approaches 1 and 3 are code patterns to adapt.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Important:</strong> <code>clip-path</code> (approach 1) clips
          <code>box-shadow</code> on the same element. If the section needs a drop shadow, use
          the <code>::before</code> approach instead.
        </p>

        <div class="demo-diagonal" role="group" aria-label="Diagonal cut live demo">
          <div class="section-skew demo-diagonal-top" data-theme="dark">
            <p class="demo-label">Preceding section</p>
            <p class="demo-section-desc">Background skewed via <code>::before</code> — content stays horizontal.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="demo-diagonal-bottom" data-theme="light">
            <p class="demo-label">Next section</p>
            <p class="demo-section-desc">Set <code>--color-section</code> to match the preceding section's background.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h4>Approach 1 — <code>clip-path</code> polygon</h4>
        <p>Simplest option. GPU-accelerated. Does not work if the section needs <code>box-shadow</code>.</p>
        <pre><code>&lt;section style="clip-path: polygon(0 0, 100% 0, 100% 92%, 0 100%);"&gt;
  &lt;!-- Section content --&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;</code></pre>

        <h4>Approach 2 — <code>::before</code> skew (shipped as <code>.section-skew</code>)</h4>
        <p>
          The background is applied to a skewed <code>::before</code> pseudo-element so the section's
          content stays horizontal and <code>box-shadow</code> on the element still works. In
          supporting browsers the angle flattens via <code>animation-timeline: view()</code> —
          from <code>skewY(-5deg)</code> to <code>skewY(0deg)</code> as the section enters the
          viewport. Static fallback for unsupported browsers: shows at
          <code>var(--diagonal-angle)</code>.
        </p>
        <pre><code>&lt;section class="section-skew"
         style="padding: 80px 0; --color-section: var(--color-bg-panel);"&gt;
  &lt;!-- Content sits horizontally above the skewed ::before background --&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;</code></pre>

        <h4>Approach 3 — SVG triangle</h4>
        <p>Precise pixel control. Place the SVG at the bottom of the preceding section.</p>
        <pre><code>&lt;section style="position: relative; padding-bottom: 60px;"&gt;
  &lt;svg viewBox="0 0 1200 60" preserveAspectRatio="none"
       style="display:block; position:absolute; bottom:0; left:0; width:100%; height:60px;"
       aria-hidden="true"&gt;
    &lt;polygon points="0,60 1200,0 1200,60" fill="var(--color-section-next)"/&gt;
  &lt;/svg&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;</code></pre>

        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Default</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>--diagonal-angle</code></td><td><code>-3deg</code></td><td>Static display angle (approach 2). Keep between <code>-2deg</code> and <code>-5deg</code>. Scroll-driven animation always starts at <code>-5deg</code> regardless of this value.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--color-section</code></td><td><code>var(--color-bg)</code></td><td>Background fill for the <code>::before</code> pseudo-element (approach 2). Override to match the actual section background.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--color-section-next</code></td><td><code>var(--color-bg-panel)</code></td><td>SVG fill colour for the triangle approach. Set to match the destination section background.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><strong>Direction carries meaning:</strong> bottom-left to top-right (<code>skewY</code> negative) reads as forward momentum. The reverse reads as stepping back. Be consistent within a page. Use at most one diagonal per scroll flow — two diagonals pointing opposite directions create visual conflict.</p>

        <!-- Stacked card reveal -->
        <h3 id="stacked-card-reveal">Stacked card reveal</h3>
        <p>
          Cinematic depth for long scrolling pages. Each section uses <code>position: sticky</code>
          to pin in the viewport; later sections slide on top of earlier ones as the user scrolls
          through the container. A static <code>transform: scale()</code> per section creates the
          physical card-deck illusion — the front (last) card is always full-size; cards behind it
          are progressively smaller.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Constraints:</strong> content inside each section must fit within <code>100dvh</code>
          — no vertical overflow. Use 3–5 sections. This pattern is its own transition; do not
          combine it with other divider patterns within the same stack container.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Critical:</strong> <code>overflow: hidden</code> on any ancestor of
          <code>.stack-container</code> will break <code>position: sticky</code> on the sections —
          the same trap as <code>.overlap-preceding</code>. Keep ancestors at <code>overflow: visible</code>
          (the default).
        </p>

        <div class="demo-stack-wrapper" role="group" aria-label="Stacked card reveal demo — scroll inside to reveal cards">
          <div class="demo-stack-inner">
            <div class="demo-stack-section" data-theme="dark"
                 style="--stack-depth: 2; --stack-index: 3">
              <p class="demo-label">Section 1</p>
              <p class="demo-section-desc">Depth 2 — behind two cards, slightly shrunk.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="demo-stack-section" data-theme="light"
                 style="--stack-depth: 1; --stack-index: 4">
              <p class="demo-label">Section 2</p>
              <p class="demo-section-desc">Depth 1 — behind one card.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="demo-stack-section" data-theme="dark" data-surface="layer"
                 style="--stack-depth: 0; --stack-index: 5">
              <p class="demo-label">Section 3 — top card</p>
              <p class="demo-section-desc">Depth 0, full-size. Scroll up to peel back.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="demo-stack-hint">↓ Scroll inside the box to reveal cards</p>

        <pre><code>&lt;div class="stack-container" style="--stack-count: 3;"&gt;
  &lt;section class="stack-section" style="background: var(--color-section-base);"&gt;
    &lt;!-- Section 1 — first in DOM, deepest in deck --&gt;
  &lt;/section&gt;
  &lt;section class="stack-section" style="background: var(--color-section-layer);"&gt;
    &lt;!-- Section 2 --&gt;
  &lt;/section&gt;
  &lt;section class="stack-section" style="background: var(--color-section-overlay);"&gt;
    &lt;!-- Section 3 — last in DOM, front of deck, full-size --&gt;
  &lt;/section&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>

        <p>
          The <code>nth-last-child</code> selectors in <code>dist/farn-components.css</code>
          automatically assign <code>--stack-depth</code> (0 = front) and
          <code>--stack-index</code> for stacks of 2–5 sections. Set them inline for larger
          stacks or to override the defaults.
        </p>
        <p>
          For sites with a fixed navigation bar, override <code>--stack-top</code> to clear
          it — e.g. <code>style="--stack-top: 64px"</code> on <code>.stack-container</code>
          or <code>:root</code>. The section height and sticky offset both adjust automatically.
        </p>

        <h4>Stacked card tokens</h4>
        <p>Tier-3 tokens pre-defined in <code>tokens/components.css</code> — override at <code>:root</code> or per instance:</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Default</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>--stack-scale-step</code></td><td><code>0.03</code></td><td>Scale reduction per depth level. 0.03 = 3% smaller per card behind the front.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--stack-top</code></td><td><code>0px</code></td><td>Sticky <code>top</code> offset. Override to clear a fixed nav bar.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--stack-radius</code></td><td><code>var(--radius-xl)</code></td><td>Top-corner radius on each section — reinforces the card metaphor.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--stack-count</code></td><td><code>4</code></td><td>Number of sections — used by <code>.stack-container</code> to compute total scroll height. Set per instance to match actual child count.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--stack-height</code></td><td><code>100vh</code></td><td>Height of each section. Set to <code>100dvh</code> to track mobile browser URL bar collapse, or a fixed value (e.g. <code>600px</code>) for non-fullscreen stacks.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Per-section variables — set via <code>nth-last-child</code> defaults or inline on each <code>.stack-section</code>:</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Default</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>--stack-depth</code></td><td>via <code>nth-last-child</code></td><td>Depth in the deck. 0 = front card (full-size); higher = further back (smaller scale). Set inline to override.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--stack-index</code></td><td>via <code>nth-last-child</code></td><td><code>z-index</code> value. Higher = renders on top. Set inline to override.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

      </div>
    </details>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Separator ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->

  <h2 id="separator">Separator <span class="badge badge-published">stable</span></h2>
  <p>
    Content-level horizontal rules — distinct from section-transition patterns. Three
